'''Thereza Imanishi-Kari''' is an associate professor of [[pathology]] at [[Tufts University]]. Her research focuses on the origins of [[autoimmune]] diseases, particularly [[systemic lupus erythematosus]], studied using [[laboratory mouse|mice]] as [[model organism]]s.<ref name="research_summary">{{cite web|title=The Thereza Imanishi-Kari Lab|url=http://sackler.tufts.edu/Faculty-and-Research/Faculty-Research-Pages/Thereza-Imanishi-Kari|website=Tufts University|access-date=4 August 2016}}</ref> Previously she had been a faculty member at the [[Massachusetts Institute of Technology]]. She is notable for her role in what became known as the "Baltimore affair", in which a 1986 paper she co-authored with [[David Baltimore]] was the subject of [[research misconduct]] allegations. Following a series of investigations, she was eventually exonerated of the charges in 1996.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The "Baltimore Case" {{!}} Institute of Bioethics {{!}} University of Miami |url=https://bioethics.miami.edu/education/timelines-project/the-baltimore-case/index.html |access-date=2025-06-11 |website=bioethics.miami.edu |language=en}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
A native of Brazil, Thereza Imanishi-Kari earned a BS degree in biology from the [[University of São Paulo]] near her home town of [[Indaiatuba, Brazil]]. Subsequently, she studied at [[Kyoto University]], in [[Kyoto, Japan]], and the [[University of Helsinki]] in Finland, which awarded her a PhD in the field of immunogenetics.<ref name=tufts>{{cite web| url=http://sackler.tufts.edu/Faculty-and-Research/Faces-of-Sackler-Faculty/Thereza-Imanishi-Kari |title=Thereza Imanishi-Kari |publisher=Sackler School of Graduate Biomedical Sciences, [[Tufts University]] |access-date=4 August 2012 }}</ref>

==Research==
Imanishi-Kari's research focuses on [[immunology]], particularly on understanding the [[molecular biology|molecular]] and [[cell biology]] underlying the development of [[autoimmune disease]]. She studies [[systemic lupus erythematosus]] using [[laboratory mouse|mouse model]]s<ref name=research_summary /> and has been funded for this work by the Lupus Research Institute and the [[National Institutes of Health]].<ref name="lri">{{cite web|title=Thereza Imanishi-Kari, PhD|url=http://www.lupusresearchinstitute.org/lupus-research/grant-recipients/imanishi-kari/thereza|website=Lupus Research Institute|access-date=4 August 2016|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160822070739/http://www.lupusresearchinstitute.org/lupus-research/grant-recipients/imanishi-kari/thereza|archive-date=22 August 2016}}</ref> Her interest in immunology was motivated in part by her sister's death due to lupus.<ref name="crotty">{{cite book|last1=Crotty|first1=Shane|title=Ahead of the Curve David Baltimore's Life in Science.|year=2001|publisher=University of California Press|location=Berkeley|isbn=9780520930261}}</ref>{{rp|149}}

==Investigation and exoneration==
In 1986, Imanishi-Kari co-authored a scientific paper on [[immunology]] with [[David Baltimore]]. The paper, published in the scientific journal ''[[Cell (journal)|Cell]]'', showed [[V(D)J recombination|
unexpected results]] on how the [[immune system]] rearranges its genes to produce [[antibody|antibodies]] against [[antigen]]s it encounters for the first time.<ref>{{cite journal |doi=10.1016/0092-8674(86)90389-2 |vauthors=Weaver D, Reis MH, Albanese C, Costantini F, Baltimore D, Imanishi-Kari T |title=Altered repertoire of endogenous immunoglobulin gene expression in transgenic mice containing a rearranged mu heavy chain gene |journal=Cell |volume=45 |issue=2 |pages=247–59 |date=April 1986 |pmid=3084104 |s2cid=26659281 }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1016/0092-8674(91)90085-D|pmid=2032282|intentional=yes}}</ref> Margot O'Toole, a researcher in Imanishi-Kari's lab, claimed she could not reproduce some of the experiments in the paper and accused Imanishi-Kari of fabricating the data. Since the research had been funded by the [[U.S. federal government]] through the [[National Institutes of Health]] (NIH), the matter was taken up by the [[United States Congress]], where it was aggressively pursued by, among others, Representative [[John Dingell]]. Largely on the basis of these findings, NIH's fraud unit, then called the [[Office of Scientific Integrity]], accused Dr. Imanishi-Kari in 1991 of falsifying data and recommended she be barred from receiving research grants for 10 years.<ref name="HHS 1996" />

In 1996, a newly constituted [[U.S. Department of Health and Human Services]] (HHS) appeals panel reviewed the case again and dismissed all charges against Imanishi-Kari.<ref name="HHS 1996">{{cite web|url=https://www.hhs.gov/dab/decisions/dab1582.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20041107115706/http://www.hhs.gov/dab/decisions/dab1582.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=7 November 2004|title=Thereza Imanishi-Kari, Ph.D., DAB No. 1582 (1996)|date=1996-06-21|publisher=[[United States Department of Health and Human Services]]|access-date=2008-12-18}}</ref> In August 1996 she gained an official position as an assistant professor in the pathology department of the [[Tufts University School of Medicine]]. There was widespread criticism of the government's system for dealing with allegations of misconduct, and calls for review of the oversight procedures dealing with the integrity of biomedical research.<ref name="Multiple Investigations">{{cite web |url=https://www.the-scientist.com/?articles.view/articleNo/18009/title/Multiple-Investigations/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151126234340/https://www.the-scientist.com/?articles.view/articleNo/18009/title/Multiple-Investigations/ |archive-date=2015-11-26 | title=Multiple Investigations |author= Billy Goodman  |date= 19 August 1996 |publisher= [[The Scientist (magazine)|The Scientist]] Magazine |access-date=2025-09-18}}</ref> The case of alleged [[scientific misconduct]] and her exoneration was reported in ''[[Scientific American]]''.<ref>{{cite journal | author = Beardsley T | year = 1996 | title = Profile: Thereza Imanishi-Kari &ndash; Starting With a Clean Slate | journal = [[Scientific American]] | volume = 275 | issue = 5| pages = 50–52 | doi=10.1038/scientificamerican1196-50}}</ref> A ''[[New York Times]]'' editorial at the time described the final result of the ten-year investigation as "embarrassment for the Federal Government and belated vindication for the accused scientist".<ref name="nyt">{{cite news|title=The Fraud Case That Evaporated|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1996/06/25/opinion/the-fraud-case-that-evaporated.html|access-date=4 August 2016|work=The New York Times|date=25 June 1996}}</ref>

The high profile of the case resulted in a great deal of published commentary on the matter. ''The New York Times'' published an account of the medical establishment's treatment of O’Toole on March 22, 1991.<ref>{{cite news|author=Philip J. Hilts|title=Biologist Who Disputed a Study Paid Dearly|newspaper=The New York Times|date=March 22, 1991 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1991/03/22/us/biologist-who-disputed-a-study-paid-dearly.html}}</ref> Mathematician [[Serge Lang]] discussed the case in an article published in the journal ''Ethics and Behavior'' in January 1993.<ref name=lang>{{cite journal|author=Lang S|title=Questions of scientific responsibility: the Baltimore case|journal=Ethics & Behavior|volume=3|issue=1 |pages=3–72 |date=January 1993 |pmid=11653082 |doi=10.1207/s15327019eb0301_1}}</ref> Several books, including ''The Baltimore Case'' (1998) by [[Daniel Kevles]] of Yale University<ref name="kevles">{{cite book|last1=Kevles|first1=Daniel J.|title=The Baltimore case : a trial of politics, science, and character.|year=2000|publisher=W.W. Norton|location=New York|isbn=0393319709|edition=1st Norton paperpack}}</ref> and ''[[The Great Betrayal: Fraud in Science]]'' by science historian [[Horace Freeland Judson]],<ref>{{cite book |author=Judson, Horace F. |title=The Great Betrayal: Fraud in Science |publisher=Harcourt |location=New York |year=2004|isbn=978-0151008773}}</ref> also covered the Baltimore affair.

